Spec: state

Purpose:
  Own the diagnostic server's one shared request state — a single @kube::Client plus one copy
    of the resolved @Config — built once at startup through @AppState::new and @Clone-d as
    cheap handles to the axum router and every @axum::extract::State, with a client-build
    failure as the only error the entry point can see before serving.

Owns:
  ./state.rs

References:
  ./config.sdd — the embedded @Config value type and the log-window defaults it carries.
  ./error.sdd — the @DiagError lane and @internal_error builder @AppState::new reports into.
  /common/src/context.sdd, /server/src/error.sdd — approved calibration references.

Must:
  The state is exactly the two fields — @kube::Client and embedded @Config — and this file
    owns no cache, informer, TTL or timer state, and no background task; no tokio or
    @std::thread spawn exists here, and the only mutable shared resource is whatever sits
    behind the client handle.
  @AppState::new is the file's only @kube::Client construction and delegates every step of
    endpoint and credential discovery to @kube::Config::infer inside @Client::try_default —
    kubeconfig first (`KUBECONFIG`, `~/.kube/config`), in-cluster service-account files plus
    `KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST`/`KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT` as fallback, the same locked-build
    behavior documented for that call in /common/src/context.sdd; this file parses no
    kubeconfig, applies no in-cluster override, and opens no API round trip — @new succeeding
    never proves the API server is reachable.
  @Clone is the plain field-wise derive: @config deep-copies all ten resolved values with no
    sharing or interior mutability, and the client copy is kube `3.1.0`'s cheap
    buffer-backed @Clone of @kube::Client — every handle issued to the router by
    `@create_router(state)` and to each request by @axum::extract::State drives the one
    client built at startup; no clone rebuilds, warms or closes a client, and clone never
    fails.
  The file installs no rustls provider: the inherited "pin `ring` before the Kubernetes
    client is built" rule from /server/server.sdd is honored one level up in ./main.rs,
    which calls @rustls::crypto::ring::default_provider().install_default() before the
    first @AppState::new call.
  The hand-written @Debug exists because the locked kube build gives @kube::Client a @Clone
    derive but no @Debug, so a derive on @AppState is impossible; it renders exactly the
    literal struct name `AppState` plus the two fields in declaration order, with the client
    masked as the `&'static str` `"kube::Client"` — client internals (endpoint, credentials,
    driver state) are structurally unreachable through this output, while the embedded
    @Config renders through its own ten-field full @Debug per ./config.sdd.

Depends on:
  @kube::Client — kube `3.1.0` locked in `/Cargo.lock` (workspace features `runtime`,
    `client`, `derive` in /Cargo.toml) imported as `Client`; only @Client::try_default and
    that build's @Clone derive of @kube::Client (its Buffer-backed handle) are used, and no
    @Debug impl for the type exists in that build — the fact forcing the manual impl.
  @crate::config::Config via ./config.rs (./config.sdd) — the by-value field and its
    @Clone/@Debug.
  @crate::error via ./error.rs (./error.sdd) — @DiagError as the error type and
    @crate::error::internal_error as the one builder called here.

Exposes:
  @AppState — `pub struct` whose only derive is @Clone; re-exported at the crate root by
    `pub use state::AppState` in ./lib.rs (the re-export is owned there). Both fields are
    `pub`, giving no type-level invariant:
    @AppState.client — @kube::Client, the Kubernetes client shared by every request.
    @AppState.config — @Config, a full copy of the resolved configuration; the log-window
      defaults `5` (@u64 hours, `log_since_hours`) and `2097152` (bytes, `log_cap_bytes`)
      from ./config.sdd travel inside every state copy and are applied only downstream —
      ./server.rs handlers pass them to ./collect/logs.rs — never by this file.
  @Debug for @AppState — hand-written impl, shape pinned in Example.
  @AppState::new — the crate's only construction path: `@pub async fn` taking `&@Config` and
    returning @Result<@Self, @DiagError>; its doc comment names @DiagError::InternalError as
    the sole failure.

Accepts:
  @AppState::new takes one `&@Config` borrow, read once and cloned into the state — no
    reference is held and the caller may keep and pass on its own copy; it accepts no
    kube-connection parameter of any kind: endpoint, credentials and namespace arrive only
    from the process environment at call time through @Client::try_default.
  @Clone accepts the state by @self, infallibly, with no precondition.
  The `pub` fields accept struct-literal construction with any client-and-config pair by any
    in-crate code, bypassing @AppState::new entirely; no such bypass exists today — ./main.rs
    after @Config::parse and the provider pin is the single construction call site.

Returns:
  @Ok of one @AppState on success: the client @Client::try_default built (unprobed against
    the cluster) and a field-equal copy of the input @Config.
  A @Clone yields a state whose @config @Debug-renders equal to the source's and whose client
    is another handle of the same built @kube::Client.
  @Debug yields `AppState { client: "kube::Client", config: Config { … } }` — the client entry
    is always that literal string, the config entry is the embedded @Config's full ten-field
    render, as pinned in Example.

Raises:
  @AppState::new has one failure shape: any @kube::Error from @Client::try_default maps
    through @internal_error to @DiagError::InternalError carrying payload
    `Failed to create kube client: {e}` — @Display `DIAG-500: Internal server error - Failed to create kube client: …`,
    @DiagError::code `DIAG-500` — with no retry, fallback client, or offline default and no
    other variant raised here; the production trigger is an environment with neither
    resolvable kubeconfig nor in-cluster variables, whose combined inference-failure @Display
    kube interpolates into the payload.
  The @Debug impl, the @Clone derive and field access are total — this file contains no
    unwrap, expect or panic path of its own; upstream ordering mistakes (a client built
    before the provider pin, inside rustls) propagate uncaught.

Handles:
  The file's single branch — @Client::try_default @Ok or @Err; the @Err @DiagError
    propagates by @Result to ./main.rs, whose exit-1 startup abort belongs to that file.
  A member the type system cannot debug — the client — is masked with a static string
    instead of skipped or shown.

Tasks:
  [ ] Cover the failure shape: @AppState::new with no resolvable kubeconfig and no in-cluster
      environment returns @Err whose @Display begins `DIAG-500: Internal server error - Failed to create kube client:`
      and whose @DiagError::code is `DIAG-500`; no test touches @AppState today.
  [ ] Cover the @Debug rendering verbatim, literal-masked `client` entry included.
  [ ] Cover @Clone: the copy's @config @Debug-renders equal to the source; client sharing is
      not directly assertable (no @PartialEq, @Debug masked), so pin only the observable half.
  [?] `client` and `config` are `pub`, so in-crate code may struct-literal an @AppState with
      any client and skip @AppState::new's error lane and single-construction path; all
      construction goes through @new from ./main.rs today. Decide later whether the fields go
      private behind accessors.
  [?] @AppState::new clones the `&@Config` while ./main.rs also hands its own owned copy to
      @run_server — two live copies with divergent drift risk; handlers read `state.config`
      while server startup reads the parameter copy. Decide later whether @Config should
      live solely in @AppState.
  [?] The hand-written @Debug has no call site in the crate — nothing renders @AppState
      through `{:?}` and axum requires no @Debug; it exists to keep the type debuggable since
      @kube::Client lacks one. Decide later whether the mask is contract or convenience.
  [?] @new validates no cluster reachability, so a wrong endpoint or stale credential shows
      up only as `500` on each first request (the kube lane of ./error.sdd) while startup
      reports success. Decide later whether a startup probe belongs here or with the caller.

Done when:
  Every Scenario below maps to a green test in `server`, the `[?]` decisions above are
    recorded, and `cargo test -p server` is green.

Scenario: Startup builds the one state
  Given @Config parsed and the `ring` provider installed in ./main.rs
  When @AppState::new runs with a kube-resolvable environment
  Then it returns @Ok of a single @AppState — the @Client::try_default client and a copy of
    that @Config — which is then cloned into the router and each request's
    @axum::extract::State
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: No cluster identity fails startup through DIAG-500
  Given an environment with neither resolvable kubeconfig nor in-cluster variables
  When @AppState::new runs
  Then it returns @Err with @Display `DIAG-500: Internal server error - Failed to create kube client: …`
    and @DiagError::code `DIAG-500`, and no @AppState is ever produced
  And the log-and-exit `1` reaction there belongs to ./main.rs
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Debug masks the client and prints the config
  Given a state over a fully defaulted @Config
  When `{:?}` renders the @AppState
  Then the `client` field shows the literal `kube::Client` and the `config` field shows the
    ten-field @Config render from the ./config.sdd Example
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Clones share the client and copy the config
  Given one @AppState handle
  When @Clone runs repeatedly, as router registration and extractors do
  Then every copy's @config @Debug-renders equal to the source's
  And all copies drive the same built @kube::Client — nothing is rebuilt, warmed or closed by
    cloning
  No mapped test exists.

Example:
  @Debug of a fully defaulted state: `AppState { client: "kube::Client", config: Config { bind: "0.0.0.0:8443", insecure_no_tls: false, tls_cert: None, tls_key: None, requestheader_ca: None, trust_request_header: false, vynil_namespace: "vynil-system", log_since_hours: 5, log_cap_bytes: 2097152, enable_packages: true } }`
  @new failure @Display shape for an unresolvable environment:
    `DIAG-500: Internal server error - Failed to create kube client: <kube inference-failure @Display>`
